  ABSTRACT = {Learning analytics has been endorsed as a potential transformative agent to support teachers in educational complexity. Learning analytics offer opportunities to personalize learning and to improve teacher decision-making, though K-12 practitioner use remains an underrepresented area in the literature. This qualitative descriptive dissertation investigated how K-12 teachers in one international-school district approached, understood and interpreted learning analytics contributing to student success using social constructivist theory and the reference model. Three key findings showed learning analytics like a power tool to increase perceived effectiveness. Teachers described the most value from integrating multiple analytics tools, and through ongoing reflection and sharing with others. This study offered a process model displaying how analytics tools may be adopted to best meet teacher needs. Examples of learning analytics application in K-12 highlighted opportunities and challenges in implementation. Recommendations for professional development and training to foster successful learning analytics implementation are provided.},
